# Third-party notices (capto-ffmpeg)

This repository builds and redistributes a **Windows `ffmpeg.exe`** configured
for Capto. The binary links **GPL** components (notably **libx264** via FFmpeg
`--enable-gpl`).

**Capto (the desktop app) may be MIT (or another license).** That does **not**
cover this sidecar binary. Ship `LICENSE` + this `NOTICE` (and/or
`licenses/`) with any redistribution of `ffmpeg.exe`.

## Components in a typical Capto build

| Component | Role in this build | Upstream license (summary) |
|-----------|--------------------|----------------------------|
| [FFmpeg](https://ffmpeg.org/) | Core binary (`n9.0` pin) | LGPL 2.1+ / **GPL 2+** when `--enable-gpl` |
| [x264](https://www.videolan.org/developers/x264.html) | Soft H.264 (`libx264`) | **GPL 2+** |
| [nv-codec-headers](https://github.com/FFmpeg/nv-codec-headers) | NVIDIA encode hooks (headers only) | MIT |
| [AMF](https://github.com/GPUOpen-LibrariesAndSDKs/AMF) | AMD encode hooks (headers only) | MIT |
| [libvpl](https://github.com/intel/libvpl) | Intel QSV dispatcher (static) | MIT |

Exact pins: see `versions.env`.

## Runtime (not redistributed here)

Hardware encoders may `LoadLibrary` vendor driver DLLs at encode time, for example:

- NVIDIA: NVENC / CUDA related DLLs
- AMD: `amfrt64.dll` (AMF runtime)
- Intel: VPL / Media SDK implementation DLLs

Those vendor runtimes are **not** shipped by this project. Users need working
GPU drivers on the machine.

